  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1025 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAnother generally calm overnight period is progressing across the\nMid-South with temperatures spanning the upper 60s to 70s. Latest\nnighttime microphysics RGB does have plenty of clouds continuing \nto filter into the region, which will aid to keep overnight lows a\nbit warmer than the past few days. By sunrise, temperatures will \nlikely be in the upper 50s to mid 60s for much of the area. A \nsomewhat dreary (spooky?) forecast does look likely for Saturday \nas a weak trough begins to move into the area. Showers will likely\nstruggle initially against the dry air and high pressure to the \neast of the area, but alas additional moisture will funnel in \nthrough the day with better precipitation chances arriving late \nSaturday and into Sunday morning. While I do not think that \nHalloween will be a washout by any means for everyone, I would \nprepare for at least some off and on rain if you are out Saturday \nevening. Precipitation will stick around through at least the \nfirst half of the day on Sunday, before moving out in the evening.\nTotal rainfall amounts will generally range across the area, with\namounts upwards of around an inch for a few locations. \n\nBy Monday, conditions will dry out temporarily across the Mid-\nSouth with WAA allowing temperatures to return to the 70s into the\nfirst half of next week. Looking to our west, an upper-level\ntrough will move off of the Rocky Mountains and will eventually\nbring us our next shot of rain into the Tuesday to Wednesday\ntimeframe. There is still some uncertainty amongst guidance with\nhow the low out of the four corners will interact with a low out\nof Canada, which will ultimately play a roll in the evolution of\nthe system. The most likely scenario at this point is a low\npulling off to our north, which would spell more of a little to no\nsevere weather setup rather than severe weather set up for our \narea. While shear will likely be on the higher side, it does look \nlike the better CAPE profiles will remain to our south. LREF \nguidance currently has a 20-30% chance of favorable conditions for\na few strong storms mostly over our far southern counties. \nHowever, given the set up, these percentages appear to be a bit \noverdone for our area. While this will be something to watch into \nnext week, the threat for severe weather does appear to be low at \nthis point. Total rainfall amounts range from 1 to 2 inches across\nthe area, which is certainly welcome given the ongoing drought \nconditions.\n\nBeyond the midweek system, LREF guidance does suggest a generally\ndry and near-normal temperature pattern will return to the Mid-\nSouth. Guidance does show a weaker upper-level low traversing the\nMidwest by the end of the week with a weak ridge developing into\nnext weekend. This combination would likely aid in allowing dry\nconditions to return and temperatures closer to normal in the\nupper 60s to low 70s.",
